In RAN1#124 it was agreed to study HARQ feedback, including HARQ disabling. Based on offline discussion during RAN1#124, some companies do not seem to be convinced of the need of HARQ disabling, stating that an implementation-based solution (transmit PDSCH of the same HARQ process ID before processing HARQ-ACK) being also feasible. The issue of HARQ enable / disable has been widely discussed in NTN in previous releases. The Rel-16 study item technical report (38.821) includes the following recommendation: The Rel-17 study on IoT NTN (36.763), however, made the following conclusion to not support HARQ disabling, pointing to an implementation-based solution to avoid HARQ stalling: Although the statement in 36.763 may be true for eMTC, the same logic may not apply to NB-IoT (this created some issues in field deployments) and may not apply to NR with current specifications as we will see next. In NR, the out of order rules may affect the throughput if HARQ disabling is not supported. Similarly, the MAC procedures (e.g. DRX) are adjusted based on whether HARQ is enabled or disabled. We present below some of the specification references below: In view of the above, and while we are open to discussing alternative solutions to HARQ disabling, we propose RAN1 agrees to support techniques to avoid HARQ stalling, taking as baseline HARQ feedback disabling. Alternative solutions can be discussed, but they should be analyzed accounting for potential impact on other procedures (e.g. DRX timers) Proposal 3: 6GR to study techniques to avoid “HARQ stalling” in scenarios where the number of HARQ processes do not allow to cover the round-trip-time with continuous scheduling. The baseline solution for this issue should be disabling HARQ feedback. Other solutions can be discussed, accounting for impact on other procedures (e.g. DRX timers, out of order rules)

